"CSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2525view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"pattern\":\"mark\"}); Total lines: 2555\nL841: If a 【520†<style-query>】 in `condition` tests the value of a property, and 【528†guarding】 a 【529†substitution context】 «\"property\", referenced-property-name» would mark it as a 【530†cyclic substitution context】, that query evaluates to false.\nL842: \nL843: For example, in --foo: if(style(--foo: bar): baz); the style() query is automatically false, since 【531†property replacement】 has already established a «\"property\", \"--foo\"» 【529†substitution context】.\nL844: If the result of `condition` is false, 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL845: \nL846:     3. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in the second 【117†<declaration-value>】 of `branch`, and return the result.\nL847: \nL848:   2. Return nothing (an empty sequence of 【168†component values】).\nL849: Note: Unlike using 【130†@media】/【131†@supports】/【135†@container】 rules, which just ignore their contents when they’re false and let the cascade determine what values otherwise apply, declarations with 【115†if()】 do not roll back the cascade if the conditions are false; any fallback values must be provided inline. However, see the 【532†revert-rule】 【484†CSS-wide keyword】.\nL1629:   * \"function\", followed by a 【768†custom function】.\nL1630: \nL1631: As 【171†substitution】 is recursively invoked by nested 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 being 【525†replaced】, 【528†guards】 \"stack up\" the 【529†substitution contexts】 passed to each invocation.\nL1632: \nL1633: When a 【529†substitution context】 is guarded, it means that, for the duration of the guard, an attempt to guard a matching substitution context again will mark all substitution contexts involved in the cycle as cyclic substitution contexts.\nL1634: For example, given the following style:\nL1635: \nL1636:     .foo {\nL1637:       --one: var(--two);\nL1638:       --two: var(--one);\nL1639:     }\nL1640: 【531†Property replacement】 for --one invokes 【171†substitution】 with a 【529†substitution context】 of «\"property\", \"--one\"». Substitution sees the var(--two) function and invokes 【525†replace a var() function】, which fetches the values of --two and substitutes again, this time with a substitution context of «\"property\", \"--two\"».\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2525view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"pattern\":\"To resolve a\"}); Total lines: 2555\nL1527: If a random() function can’t be fully simplified by 【173†computed value】 time, then its arguments are maximally simplified, and its specified 【123†<random-key>】 is replaced with fixed BASE, where BASE is the function’s random base value.\nL1528: Note: As an 【126†arbitrary substitution function】, 【122†random-item()】 is always replaced at 【173†computed value】 time.\nL1529: \nL1530: For example, given the declaration 【690†width: random(100px, 100%)】, the calculations can’t be simplified at 【173†computed value】 time because the percentage depend on 【435†used value】 information (the size of the element’s 【786†containing block】, to resolve the 100% against).\nL1531: So, the 【173†computed value】 of the property becomes something like 【690†width: random(fixed .1234, 100px, 100%)】. Once the 100% is resolved to a length (say, 500px), the function will be able to fully simplify (to 149.36px).\nL1532: ## 10. Tree Counting Functions: the 【787†sibling-count()】 and 【725†sibling-index()】 notations\nL1533: \nL1534: Tests\nL1535:   * 【788†calc-sibling-function-in-shadow-dom.html†wpt.fyi】 【789†(live test)†wpt.live】 【790†(source)†github.com】\nL1536:   * 【791†calc-sibling-function-parsing.html†wpt.fyi】 【792†(live test)†wpt.live】 【793†(source)†github.com】\nL1537:   * 【794†calc-sibling-function.html†wpt.fyi】 【795†(live test)†wpt.live】 【796†(source)†github.com】\nL1538:   * 【797†sibling-function-container-query-invalidation.html†wpt.fyi】 【798†(live test)†wpt.live】 【799†(source)†github.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Color Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-5/)\nciteturn2525view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view2\",\"pattern\":\"The computed value of light-dark\"}); Total lines: 2221\nL139: An absolute color is a 【100†<color>】 whose computed value has an absolute, colorimetric interpretation. This means that the value is not:\nL140: \nL141:   * 【119†currentColor】 (which depends on the value of the 【120†color】 property)\nL142: \nL143:   * a 【103†<system-color>】 (which depends on the color mode)\nL144: \nL145:   * 【105†<light-dark-color>】 (which depends on the color mode)\nL146: \nL147:   * 【96†<contrast-color()>】 (which depends on the color mode)\nL148: \nL149:   * 【104†<device-cmyk()>】 (which has no colorimetric basis)\nL416: 【104†device-cmyk()】 can be used in 【97†color-mix()】 but the result will depend on how the implementation chooses to obtain a computed value.\nL417: \nL418:     color-mix(in lab, device-cmyk(0.091777 0.043303 0.312816 0.000000) 100%, yellow);\nL419: \nL420: \nL421: Since the first color is at 100%, the second color is 0% and does not affect the mixed result in any way. The result is thus the computed value of the first color, in CIE Lab.\nL1215:   1. If the user, author, or user-agent stylesheet has an 【317†@color-profile】 definition for device-cmyk, and the resource specified by the src descriptor can be retrieved, and the resource is a valid CMYK ICC profile, and the user agent can process ICC profiles, the computed value of the 【104†device-cmyk()】 function must be the Lab value of the CMYK color.\nL1216:   2. Otherwise, the computed value of the 【104†device-cmyk()】 function must be the sRGB value of the CMYK color, as converted with the following naive conversion algorithm.\nL1217: For example, with no 【317†@color-profile】, the following colors are equivalent, using the naive conversion.\nL1218: \nL1219:     color:  device-cmyk(0 81% 81% 30%);\nL1220:     color:  rgb(178 34 34);\nL1221:     color:  firebrick;\nL1222: \nL1223: \nL1224: With the 【317†@color-profile】 specified as in the example stylesheet, the following colors are equivalent, using colorimetric conversion.\nL1225: \nL1226:     color:  device-cmyk(0 81% 81% 30%);\nL1227:     color:  lab(45.060% 45.477 35.459)\nL1228:     color:  rgb(70.690% 26.851% 19.724%);\nL1250:   * `black = 1 - max(red, green, blue)`\nL1251:   * `cyan = (1 - red - black) / (1 - black), or 0 if black is 1`\nL1252:   * `magenta = (1 - green - black) / (1 - black), or 0 if black is 1`\nL1253:   * `yellow = (1 - blue - black) / (1 - black), or 0 if black is 1`\nL1254:   * alpha is the same as the input color\nL1255: ## 7. Reacting to the used color-scheme: the 【98†light-dark()】 Function\nL1256: \nL1257: 【344†System colors】 have the ability to react to an element’s 【345†color scheme】. The 【98†light-dark()】 function exposes the same capability to authors.\nL1258: There are two forms of this function: one takes a pair of colors while the other takes a pair of images. Attempting to use one image and one color will result in a parse-time error.\nL1259: \nL1260:     light-dark() = 【105†<light-dark-color>】 【102†|】 【346†<light-dark-image>】\nL1261:     【105†<light-dark-color>】 = light-dark(【100†<color>】【132†,】 【100†<color>】)\nL1262:     【346†<light-dark-image>】 = light-dark( [ 【347†<image>】 【102†|】 none ] 【132†,】 [ 【347†<image>】 【102†|】 none ] )\nL1263: For the color form, this function computes to the computed value of the first color, if the 【348†element color scheme】 is 【349†light】, or to the computed value of the second color, if the element color scheme is 【350†dark】.\nL1264: \nL1265: For the image form, this function returns the first image, if the 【348†element color scheme】 is 【349†light】, or the second image, if the element color scheme is 【350†dark】.\nL1266: \nL1267: The none keyword computes to image(transparent) (a fully transparent image with no natural size).\nL1268: For example, to maintain a legible contrast on links, for light mode and dark mode:\nL1269: \nL1270:       a:link {\nL1271:           color: light-dark(blue, #81D9FE);\nL1272:           background-color: light-dark(white, black);\nL1273:     }\nL1274: \nL1275: \nL1276: The traditional blue link text is legible on a white background (WCAG contrast 8.59:1, AAA pass) but would not be legible on a black background (WCAG contrast 2.44:1, AA fail). Instead, a lighter blue #81D9FE is used in dark mode. (WCAG contrast 13.28:1, AAA pass).\nL1277: \nL1278: Legible link text\nL1279: Illegible link text\nL1280: \nL1281: Legible link text\nL1282: \nL1283: For example, to provide easily visible list bullets for light mode and dark mode:\nL1284: \nL1285:     ul.fancy {\nL1286:       list-style-image: light-dark(\nL1287:         url(\"icons/deep-maroon-ball.png\"),\nL1288:         url(\"icons/pale-yellow-star.png\")\nL1289:       );\nL1290:     }\nL1291: \nL1292: \nL1293: For example, a raster image is used in light mode, while in dark mode we want a fully-transparent image.\nL1294: \nL1295:     background-image: light-dark(url(my-light-image.png), none);\nL1296: This is equivalent to:\nL1297: \nL1298:     background-image: light-dark(url(my-light-image.png), image(transparent));\nL1299: \nL1300: \nL1301: Tests\nL1302:   * 【351†light-dark-basic.html†wpt.fyi】 【352†(live test)†wpt.live】 【353†(source)†github.com】\nL1303:   * 【354†light-dark-currentcolor.html†wpt.fyi】 【355†(live test)†wpt.live】 【356†(source)†github.com】\nL1304:   * 【357†light-dark-image.html†wpt.fyi】 【358†(live test)†wpt.live】 【359†(source)†github.com】\nL1305:   * 【360†light-dark-image-none-interpolation.html†wpt.fyi】 【361†(live test)†wpt.live】 【362†(source)†github.com】\nL1306:   * 【363†light-dark-image-none.html†wpt.fyi】 【364†(live test)†wpt.live】 【365†(source)†github.com】\nL1307:   * 【366†light-dark-inheritance.html†wpt.fyi】 【367†(live test)†wpt.live】 【368†(source)†github.com】\nL1308:   * 【369†light-dark-currentcolor-in-color.html†wpt.fyi】 【370†(live test)†wpt.live】 【371†(source)†github.com】\nL1309:   * 【372†highlight-styling-004.html†wpt.fyi】 【373†(live test)†wpt.live】 【374†(source)†github.com】\nL1310:   * 【375†light-dark-animation-crash.html†wpt.fyi】 【376†(live test)†wpt.live】 【377†(source)†github.com】\nL1311: ## 8. Dynamically specifying a text color with adequate contrast: the 【96†contrast-color()】 Function\nL1312: \nL1313: When colors are created dynamically, it can often be a challenge to specify a text color that provides adequate contrast with them when used as a background color. The 【96†contrast-color()】 function automatically provides a color with guaranteed color contrast when used as a text color on a solid background of the specified color.\nL1345: If all 【100†<color>】 parameters resolve to the corresponding colors in their respective color spaces, the computed value is the mixed color, in the specified mixing color space, resolved according to 【419†CSS Color 4 §  15. Resolving <color> Values】. Otherwise (if 【119†currentColor】 was used in the function), the computed value is the 【97†color-mix()】 function with each <color> parameter resolved according to 【419†CSS Color 4 §  15.\nL1353: ### 10.2. Resolving Relative Color Syntax Values\nL1354: \nL1355: If all 【100†<color>】 parameters resolve to the corresponding colors in their respective color spaces, the computed value is the absolute <color> value, in the 【191†relative color processing space】, resolved according to 【419†CSS Color 4 §  15. Resolving <color> Values】.\nL1356: \nL1357: Tests\nL1361: Otherwise (if 【119†currentColor】 was used in the function), the computed value is the Relative Color Syntax function with the origin 【100†<color>】 parameter resolved according to 【419†CSS Color 4 §  15. Resolving <color> Values】, thus preserving inheritance into child elements.\nL1362: \nL1363: Tests\nL1416: The serialization of the declared value of a 【97†color-mix()】 function is the string \"color-mix(\", followed by, unless the 【129†<color-space>】 is 【416†oklab】 (whether explicitly specified or defaulted): the string \"in \", followed by the <color-space> in all-lowercase, followed by, if a 【156†<hue-interpolation-method>】 was specified and is not shorter hue, \" \" and the <hue-interpolation-method> in all-lowercase, followed by \", \", followed by the serialization of each color argument (see below), separated by \", \", followed by \")\".\nL1417: Each color argument is serialized as the serialized 【100†<color>】, followed by, if a percentage is serialized for this argument (see below), \" \" and the serialized percentage.\nL1418: \nL1419: Each color argument is serialized individually; in particular, identical colors are not collapsed into a single argument.\nL1420: \nL1421: The serialized percentages of the declared value of a 【97†color-mix()】 function are determined as follows. Let `N` be the number of color arguments.\nL1422: \nL1423: For each argument, let its effective percentage be:\nL1442: The serialized declared value of\nL1522:   4. for 【118†alpha()】\nL1523: \nL1524:   * the string \"alpha(from \", followed by the 【60†serialization of the origin color】 using the rules for serializing nested origin colors, followed by a single space, followed by the canonical colorspace (\"xyz-d65\" for \"xyz\") in all-lowercase, followed by \" / \" and the alpha component as specified (using the same rules as the color channel arguments, but clamped) followed by \")\".\nL1525: \nL1526: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1527: \nL1528:     OkLcH(from peru  l    c  h)\nL1529: is the string \"oklch(from peru l c h)\"\nL1530: \nL1531: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1532: \nL1533:     rgb(from red calc(r / 2) g calc(30%));\nL1534: \nL1535: is the string \"rgb(from red calc(0.5 * r) g calc(30%))\", while the serialization of the computed value is the string \"color(srgb 0.5 0 0.3)\".\nL1536: \nL1537: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1538: \nL1539:     hsl(from hsl(0deg 10% 50%) h s l);\nL1540: is the string \"hsl(from hsl(0deg 10% 50%) h s l)\", while the serialization of the computed value is the string \"color(srgb 0.55 0.45 0.45)\".\nL1541: \nL1542: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1543: \nL1544:     hsl(from hsl(none 10% 50%) h s l);\nL1545: \nL1546: is the string \"hsl(from hsl(none 10% 50%) h s l)\", while the serialization of the computed value is the string \"hsl(none 10% 50%)\".\nL1547: The computed value carries forward the 【441†missing】 hue, giving an 【111†hsl()】 【197†relative color】 whose hue is itself missing. Because the resolved value contains missing color components, the serialization uses the modern hsl() form, yielding the string \"hsl(none none none / none)\" rather than color(srgb 0.55 0.45 0.45).\nL1548: \nL1549: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1550: \nL1551:     hsl(from rebeccapurple none none none / none);\nL1552: \nL1553: is the string \"hsl(from rebeccapurple none none none / none)\".\nL1554: The computed value carries forward the 【441†missing】 alpha (alpha is its own 【195†analogous component】), giving an 【111†hsl()】 【197†relative color】 whose hue, saturation, lightness, and alpha are all missing. Because the resolved value contains missing color components, the serialization uses the modern hsl() form, yielding the string \"hsl(none none none / none)\" rather than color(srgb 0 0 0 / none).\nL1555: \nL1556: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1557: \nL1558:     hsl(from hsl(127.9 302% 25.33%) h s l);\nL1559: is the string \"hsl(from hsl(127.9 302% 25.33%) h s l)\", while the serialization of the computed value is the string \"color(srgb -0.511666 1.018266 -0.310225)\".\nL1560: \nL1561: Given the following HTML (note the color property set on this element):\nL1562: \nL1563:     <div id=\"example\"\nL1564:       style=\"background-color: rgb(from currentcolor r g calc(b / 2));\nL1565:       color: blue;\">\nL1566:     </div>\nL1567: The serialization of the declared value of background-color is the string \"rgb(from currentcolor r g calc(b / 2))\" while the serialization of the computed value is the string \"color(srgb 0 0 0.5)\"\nL1568: \nL1569: For example, the serialization of the declared value of\nL1570: \nL1571:     alpha(from red / 0.5)\nL1572: \nL1573: is the string \"color(srgb 1 0 0 / 0.5)\" because trailing zeros are trimmed, while the serialization of the declared value of\nL1574: \nL1575:     alpha(from gold / calc(2/3))\nL1576: is the string \"color(srgb 1 0.843137 0 / 0.666667)\" because, by 【449†CSS Color 4 § 16.1.2 Serializing modern alpha values】 modern alpha values are serialized with six decimal places (unless there are trailing zeroes).\nL1577: The serialization of the result of a relative color function depends on whether the keyword 【119†currentColor】 is the 【192†origin color】. If so, the result is serialized as the declared value. This allows the correct value to be used on child elements whose color property has a different value. Otherwise, it is the resolved value, which is a 【100†<color>】, as defined in 【436†CSS Color 4 §  16. Serializing <color> Values】.\nL1578: \nL1579: The form used depends on the 【191†relative color processing space】:\nL1580: 【191†relative color processing space】  | form\nL1581: --- | ---\nL1582: srgb  | color(srgb r g b)\nL1583: srgb-linear  | color(srgb-linear r g b)\nL1584: display-p3  | color(display-p3 r g b)\nL1585: a98-rgb  | color(a98-rgb r g b)\nL1586: prophoto-rgb  | color(prophoto-rgb r g b)\nL1587: rec2020  | color(rec2020 r g b)\nL1588: hsl  | color(srgb r g b)\nL1589: hwb  | color(srgb r g b)\nL1590: xyz-d65  | color(xyz-d65 x y z)\nL1591: xyz-d50  | color(xyz-d50 x y z)\nL1592: xyz  | color(xyz-d65 x y z)\nL1593: lab  | lab(l a b)\nL1594: lch  | lch(l c h)\nL1595: oklab  | oklab(l a b)\nL1596: oklch  | oklch(l c h)\nL1597: However, if the resolved value of an 【111†hsl()】 or 【113†hwb()】 【197†relative color】 has at least one 【441†missing color component】 (including a missing alpha 【196†carried forward】 from the 【192†origin color】 per 【194†CSS Color 4 § 13.3 Interpolating with Missing Components】), the form used is the modern hsl(h s l / a) or hwb(h w b / a) syntax respectively, preserving the original color function and each 【200†none】 value, rather than degrading to color(srgb r g b) (which would lose the 【185†hsl】/【417†hwb】 identity).\nL1598: This parallels 【60†§ 11.2 Serializing Origin Colors】, which always emits the modern slash syntax for origin colors, and follows the general sRGB serialization rules in 【442†CSS Color 4 § 16.2.2 CSS serialization of sRGB values】.\nL1599: Tests\nL1600: \nL1601:   * 【450†color-valid-relative-color-opaque-alpha.html†wpt.fyi】 【451†(live test)†wpt.live】 【452†(source)†github.com】\nL1602: \nL1603: The minimum precision for round-tripping is the same as that specified in 【453†CSS Color 4 § 16.5 Serializing values of the color() function】.\nL1604: \nL1605: The result of serializing\nL1606: \nL1607:     lch(from peru calc(l * 0.8) calc(c * 0.7) calc(h + 180))\nL1608: \nL1609: is the string \"lch(49.80224 37.80819 243.6803)\"\nL1610: ### 11.4. Serializing Custom Color Spaces\nL1611: \nL1612: The precision with which 【99†color()】 component values are retained, and thus the number of significant figures in the serialized value, is not defined in this specification, but for CMYK color spaces must at least be sufficient to round-trip values with eight bit precision; this will result in at least two decimal places unless trailing zeroes have been omitted.\nL1613: The serialized value of the color in\nL1614: \nL1615:     @color-profile --swop5c {\nL1616:         src: url('https://example.org/SWOP2006_Coated5v2.icc');\nL1617:         }\nL1618:         .header {\nL1619:         background-color:    color(--swop5c  0% 70.0% 20.00% .0%);\nL1620:         }\nL1621: \nL1622: is the string \"color(--swop5c 0 0.7 0.2 0)\"\nL1623: ### 11.5. Serializing device-cmyk Values\nL1624: \nL1625: The serialized form of 【104†device-cmyk()】 values is derived from the 【454†computed value】 and uses the device-cmyk() form, with lowercase letters for the function name.\nL1626: \nL1627: The component values are serialized in base 10, as 【199†<number>】. A single ASCII space character \" \" must be used as the separator between the component values.\nL1628: Trailing fractional zeroes in any component values must be omitted; if the fractional part consists of all zeroes, the decimal point must also be omitted.\nL1629: \nL1630: The serialized value of the color\nL1631: \nL1632:       device-cmyk(0 81% 81% 30%)\nL1633: \nL1634: is the string \"device-cmyk(0 0.81 0.81 0.3)\"\nL1698:   * Added a backlink from Color Interpolation in this specification, to the same section in CSS Color 4 where most of this is defined (【469†Issue 13788†github.com】)\nL1699:   * Added a second form of the light-dark() function, which takes a pair of images rather than a pair of colors (【470†Issue 12513†github.com】)\nL1700:   * Added a \"none\" option to the image form of light-dark() (【470†Issue 12513†github.com】)\nL1701:   * Added a color-mix() example with three colors, now that it is no longer restricted to just two.\nL1702:   * Updated color-mix() serialization: omit color space when it is the default (oklab), serialize hue interpolation method when non-default, generalize percentage rules for N colors (omit all when equal to 100%/N, otherwise serialize all), and clarify that identical colors are not collapsed (【471†Issue 13320†github.com】)\nL1703:   * Improve visibility of the constraint that shorter is the default he interpolation method\nL1704:   * Placed Custom Color Spaces, '@color-profile', 'device-cmyk()', Relative Alpha Colors at-risk\nL1705:   * Preserved hsl/hwb identity when result contains none (【472†Issue 10254†github.com】)\nL1706:   * Changed the computed value of light-dark() none from linear-gradient(transparent) to image(transparent) (【473†Issue 13897†github.com】)\nL1707:   * Reworked the color-scheme section to use the concepts of 'page color scheme' and 'element color scheme' (【474†PR 13857†github.com】) (【475†Issue 13377†github.com】) (【476†Issue 7213†github.com】) (【477†Issue 7493†github.com】)\nL1708:   * Guarded against division by zero in color-mix() algorithm (【468†Issue 14013†github.com】)\nL1709:   * Removed special casing of 100% from the color-mix() algorithm, thus avoiding a discontinuity near fully-transparent colors (【466†Issue 14014†github.com】)\nL1710:   * Defined the term relative color processing space (【478†Issue 13394†github.com】) (【479†Issue 13392†github.com】)\nL1711:   * Serialization of relative colors (including the alpha-only form) depends on the relative color processing space (【479†Issue 13992†github.com】)\nL1712:   * Defined serialization of the declared value of the alpha() function (【479†Issue 13992†github.com】)\nL1713:   * Defined resolving of relative colors in terms of the relative color processing space, thus using modern syntax (【478†Issue 13394†github.com】)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn2525view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view0\",\"pattern\":\"computationally independent\"}); Total lines: 1339\nL345:      * 【86†parse†drafts.csswg.org】 `initialValue` according to `【139†<declaration-value>†drafts.csswg.org】?` if `syntax definition` is the 【97†universal syntax definition】, and according to `syntax definition` otherwise.\nL346: \nL347:      * If parsing returns failure, 【155†throw†webidl.spec.whatwg.org】 a `【156†SyntaxError†webidl.spec.whatwg.org】` and exit this algorithm. Otherwise, let `parsed initial value` be the parsed result.\nL348:      * If `parsed initial value` is not 【159†computationally independent】, 【155†throw†webidl.spec.whatwg.org】 a `【156†SyntaxError†webidl.spec.whatwg.org】` and exit this algorithm.\nL349: \nL350:   5. Set `inherit flag` to the value of `inherits`.\nL351:   6. Let `registered property` be a 【82†struct†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 with a property name of `name`, a syntax of `syntax definition`, an initial value of `parsed initial value`, and an inherit flag of `inherit flag`. 【160†Append†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 `registered property` to `property set`.\nL352: \nL353: A property value is computationally independent if it can be converted into a computed value using only the value of the property on the element, and \"global\" information that cannot be changed by CSS.\nL354: For example, 5px is 【159†computationally independent】, as converting it into a computed value doesn’t change it at all. Similarly, 1in is 【159†computationally independent】, as converting it into a computed value relies only on the \"global knowledge\" that 1in is 96px, which can’t be altered or adjusted by anything in CSS.\nL355: On the other hand, 3em is not 【159†computationally independent】, because it relies on the value of 【124†font-size†drafts.csswg.org】 on the element (or the element’s parent). Neither is a value with a 【89†var()†drafts.csswg.org】 function, because it relies on the value of a 【77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org】.\nL356: When a custom property is registered with a given type, the process via which specified values for that property are turned into computed values is defined fully by the type selected, as described in 【25†§ 2.4 Computed Value-Time Behavior】.\nL357: \nL358: Note: A way to unregister properties may be added in the future.\nL359: Registering a custom property must not affect the 【161†cascade†drafts.csswg.org】 in any way. Regardless of what syntax is specified for a registered property, at parse time it is still parsed as normal for a 【77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org】, accepting nearly anything.\nL360: If the 【90†specified value†drafts.csswg.org】 for a 【81†registered custom property】 violates the registered syntax, however, the property becomes 【98†invalid at computed-value time†drafts.csswg.org】 (and thus resets to the registered initial value).\nL361: By default, all custom property declarations that can be parsed as a sequence of tokens are valid. Hence, the result of this stylesheet:\nL362: \nL363:     .thing {\nL364:       --my-color: green;\nL365:       --my-color: url(\"not-a-color\");\nL366:       color: var(--my-color);\nL367:     }\nL368: is to set the 【162†color†drafts.csswg.org】 property of elements of class \"thing\" to 【92†inherit†drafts.csswg.org】. The second --my-color declaration overrides the first at parse time (both are valid), and the 【89†var()†drafts.csswg.org】 reference in the 【162†color†drafts.csswg.org】 property is found to be 【163†invalid at computed-value time†www.w3.org】 (because url(\"not-a-color\") is not a color).\nL872:   * 【159†computationally independent】, in § 4.1\nL873:   * 【199†consume a data type name】, in § 5.4.4\nL874:   * 【195†consume a syntax component】, in § 5.4.3\nL875:   * 【135†consume a syntax definition】, in § 5.4.2\nL876:   * 【206†CSSPropertyRule】, in § 6.1\nL877:   * 【96†custom property registration】, in § 2.1\nL878:   * 【188†data type name】, in § 5.4.1\nL879:   * 【239†equivalent token sequence】, in § 2.7\nL880:   * inherits\nL881:     * 【213†attribute for CSSPropertyRule】, in § 6.1\nL882:     * 【136†descriptor for @property】, in § 3.2\n"
